  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...    \nEnsembles remain in good agreement that the subtropical high over NM \nwill retrograde into AZ and strengthen, especially by Friday- \nSaturday, with mean H5 heights building to a 599-601 dam range, near \nor just above CFSR climatological maximum values. NAEFS/ENS mean 850 \nmb temperatures will also approach climatological maximum values \naround 32C-35C. Under drier conditions, much clearer skies thanks to \nreduced thunderstorm activity, and strong anticyclonic subsidence, \nheights/temperatures aloft in this range will support afternoon \nsurface temperatures peaking between 115F-120F, with values on the \nhigher end of that range favored in Southeast CA. Saturday is \nshaping up to be the hottest day, with NBM advertising 25-40% \nprobabilities of reaching 120+F in some of the hottest spots across \nSoutheast CA and along the lower Colorado River Valley. Overnight \nlows are also forecast to warm into the upper 80s to lower 90s, \nproviding very little relief from the heat. Coverage of Major \nHeatRisk builds starting Thursday and is widespread Friday-Sunday, \nwith areas of Extreme HeatRisk indicated in those areas where \novernight lows do not cool below 90F. As such, widespread Extreme \nHeat Warnings go into effect for the entire forecast area Thursday \nand will continue through the weekend. \n\nLong range global guidance continues to indicate moisture increasing \nacross the area as the subtropical high weakens late this weekend \ninto the following week. However, details are very uncertain this \nfar out. Current consensus of the GEFS and ENS is for the northern \nstream to impinge further south, flattening the subtropical ridge. \nmeanwhile, flow aloft on the southern periphery of the subtropical \nanticyclone strengthens and turns easterly to northeasterly, \nexposing the region to the possibility of easterly waves and better \nshear/steering flow to carry storms off Eastern/Southeastern AZ \nterrain features. Regardless of the eventual increase in moisture \nand thunderstorm activity, this will also help end the extended \nperiod of extreme heat conditions by early next week.",
